Curious structure for this new $DCAP ETF, an actively managed fund investing in preferred securities issued by Bitcoin treasury companies.

At launch, it expects to be roughly 50/50 $STRC and Strive’s own $SATA preferred.

Strive is the sub-adviser but can’t provide research on SATA and has no authority to make investment decisions for the fund. 

So Strive is sub-advising a fund that invests heavily in Strive, while being walled off from advising on Strive 🤔

@Aaleks_crypt Then why are they sub-advising?

@villamilalx competitor lol

Fair question! At launch you can obviously replicate the ~50/50 exposure yourself. The proposed value-add is active management: writing puts, adding leverage when the preferreds dislocate, and eventually allocating across a broader universe as more digital credit gets issued.

The unusual part is that Strive is the sub-adviser while its own $SATA is ~50% of the fund. They address that conflict by giving Tuttle all investment discretion and prohibiting Strive from even providing research on SATA. But it does make the structure a little odd.

